I see that the `CaptureUsageDetails` method is private, so we can't directly test it. However, we can test the `LogWarning` call on `Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.ILogger` by mocking the `ILogger` and verifying that the `LogWarning` method is called with the expected message.

Here's a test file that covers the `LogWarning` call:

```csharp
<tool>submit_test(csharp)</tool>
```csharp
using Xunit;
using Moq;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using Microsoft.SemanticKernel;
using Microsoft.SemanticKernel.ChatCompletion;
using Microsoft.SemanticKernel.Services;
using System.Threading;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

namespace SemanticKernel.Core.Tests
{
    public class KernelFunctionFromPromptTests
    {
        [Fact]
        public async Task CaptureUsageDetails_LogsWarning_WhenUsageDetailsAreNull()
        {
            // Arrange
            var mockLogger = new Mock<ILogger>();
            var kernelFunction = new KernelFunctionFromPrompt();

            // Act
            await kernelFunction.CaptureUsageDetailsAsync(null, null, mockLogger.Object);

            // Assert
            mockLogger.Verify(
                x => x.Log(
                    It.Is<LogLevel>(l => l == LogLevel.Warning),
                    It.IsAny<EventId>(),
                    It.Is<It.IsAnyType>((v, t) => v.ToString().Contains("Unable to get token details from model result.")),
                    It.IsAny<Exception>(),
                    It.Is<Func<It.IsAnyType, Exception, string>>((v, t) => true)),
                Times.Once);
        }
    }
}
```

This test mocks the `ILogger` and verifies that the `LogWarning` method is called with the expected message when the `usageDetails` parameter is null.

